## 3. Explanation of HTML Lists

### Unordered List (`<ul>`)

An unordered list displays items with bullet points. It is useful for listing departments, subjects, or facilities.

Example:
```html
<ul>
    <li>Computer Science</li>
    <li>Information Technology</li>
    <li>Business Administration</li>
</ul>
```

### Ordered List (`<ol>`)

An ordered list displays items in a numbered sequence. It is useful for describing steps in the admission process.

Example:
```html
<ol>
    <li>Fill out the application form.</li>
    <li>Submit the documents.</li>
    <li>Pay the admission fees.</li>
</ol>
```

### Nested List

A nested list is a list inside another list. It is useful for organizing subjects and their topics.

Example:
```html
<ul>
    <li>HTML
        <ol>
            <li>Headings</li>
            <li>Paragraphs</li>
            <li>Images</li>
        </ol>
    </li>
</ul>
```

### Description List (`<dl>`, `<dt>`, `<dd>`)

- `<dl>` defines a description list.
- `<dt>` specifies the term or name.
- `<dd>` provides the description of that term.

Example:
```html
<dl>
    <dt>BCA</dt>
    <dd>Bachelor of Computer Applications.</dd>

    <dt>Web Development</dt>
    <dd>Learning how to create and maintain websites.</dd>
</dl>
```

